> Hermes uses `.hermes.md` (or `HERMES.md`) at the project root as the
> persistent behavioral instruction surface — it is concatenated into
> the system prompt at session start alongside `~/.hermes/SOUL.md`.
> The persona stays in SOUL.md; this file adds the MPM behavioral layer.
> Edit the canonical protocol at
> `~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md` rather than this file for
> behavioral changes.

## MPM behavioral contract — host-independent

MPM is the persistent agent substrate for cross-session continuity on
this machine. The full behavioral contract is canonical at
`~/.mpm/agent_installation/mpm-agent-protocol.md`. The non-negotiable invariants
that this host adapter inherits:

1. **Wake on session start.** Read MPM wake context (`mode`, `persona`,
   active `topics`, recent memories, open work, last handoff) before
   answering substantive questions. Hermes surfaces this through
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_context` action `read_wake_context`. Calling it is
   mandatory, not optional, at the start of every session. The persona
   stays in `~/.hermes/SOUL.md` — wake protocol and persona are
   distinct slots.

2. **Persist during work, not only at the end.** Use the appropriate
   MPM tool (`mpm_memory`, `mpm_decisions`, `mpm_lessons`,
   `mpm_topics`, `mpm_references`) for any durable knowledge a future
   session would otherwise have to rediscover. The `mpm-mcp` server
   stamps provenance automatically via the env vars wired in
   `~/.hermes/config.yaml` (`MPM_WORKSPACE` resolves the canonical
   binary and DB).

3. **Skill discovery before reinventing.** When the current task
   context suggests a previously learned procedure would apply, query
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_context` action `proactive_recall_hint` (params:
   `conversation_text`) and read each surfaced skill via
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_skills` action `read` (params: `name`). The wake
   payload already includes a bounded `<available_skills>`
   catalogue — discovery adds a context-driven filter on top. Don't
   auto-scan every turn; invoke when context suggests a remembered
   procedure would apply. Reactive catalog fallback via
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_skills` action `list` (params: `scope`).

4. **Handoff before genuine session closure.** Before sending a turn
   that closes the session, run the handoff check. If substantial work
   has occurred since the last handoff, write it via
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_handoff` action `write` (params: `session_id`,
   `summary`, `state`, `commitments`, `open_questions`, `note`). Use
   `mcp__mpm__mpm_scratchpad` actions `flush`/`read`/`discard`/`promote`
   (params: `session_id`, `thesis`, `supporting`) for in-session
   volatile working state. Wake is how future-me starts; handoff is
   how future-me receives the previous session.

   Mid-session acknowledgements (`ok`, `thanks`, `ty`, `ack`) are NOT
   session-closing. Do not write a handoff on every chat ack — write
   one when substantial work has happened or a session-closing
   trigger fires.

5. **MPM is the source of truth for cross-session continuity.**
   Anything that should survive across sessions goes to MPM, not just
   transient conversation context.

6. **Recovery / fallback.** If the MCP server is unavailable or
   `mcp__mpm__*` tools are missing (e.g., the `mpm-mcp` process is
   killed or the env var resolution failed), fall back to the CLI
   path: `mpm call <tool> --payload '{"action":"<op>","params":{...}}'`.
   Do not abandon persistence when the preferred integration breaks.

## Hermes-specific notes

The `mpm` skill at `~/.hermes/skills/mpm/SKILL.md` (auto-loaded when
MPM is in scope) covers operational surface — wiring, registry, prime
directives. This `.hermes.md` covers the **behavioral** layer (wake,
persist during work, handoff discipline, recovery) the skill does not
own, because the skill is loaded on task relevance, not at session
start unconditionally.

Hermes's `load_soul_md` reads the entire `SOUL.md` file with no
managed-block convention; do not duplicate the canonical protocol
into SOUL.md. Persona stays in SOUL.md; protocol lives here.

Hermes does NOT have a managed-block convention (unlike Claude Code's
`<!-- BEGIN/END MPM-MANAGED SECTION -->` markers). This installer uses
a header comment block as a stable anchor for detection and re-install;
the file remains plain markdown between the header markers.
